<a class="country-hub-card" href="/food-crisis-by-country/united-kingdom/"><img class="country-hub-thumb" src="/images/articles/uk-food-shortage.jpg" alt="British supermarket fresh-food aisle with produce and everyday grocery staples." loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="country-card-tag">Defra supply data · 2025</span><h2>United Kingdom</h2><p>How domestic production, diverse imports and official supply statements help put food-shortage rumours in context.</p><span class="country-card-link">Read UK briefing →</span></a>
<a class="country-hub-card" href="/food-crisis-by-country/canada/"><img class="country-hub-thumb" src="/images/articles/canada-food-shortage.jpg" alt="Canadian grocery produce section with fruit, vegetables and everyday staples." loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="country-card-tag">Statistics Canada · Aug 2026</span><h2>Canada</h2><p>Official grocery-price changes, local stockout claims and practical preparedness for ordinary disruptions.</p><span class="country-card-link">Read Canada briefing →</span></a>
<a class="country-hub-card" href="/food-crisis-by-country/australia/"><img class="country-hub-thumb" src="/images/articles/australia-food-shortage.jpg" alt="Australian supermarket refrigerated food section with chilled products and fresh groceries." loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="country-card-tag">ACSC alert · 14 Oct 2026</span><h2>Australia</h2><p>What the French supermarket freezer incident means for Australia—and what the cyber-security agency actually said.</p><span class="country-card-link">Read Australia briefing →</span></a></div><section class="country-hub-method"><h2>Questions people ask when headlines feel alarming</h2><p>Want to know whether a supermarket freezer cyberattack means food will run out, or whether you should buy extra groceries? Start with these evidence-led explainers: <a href="/articles/can-hackers-spoil-supermarket-food/">what the 2026 refrigeration incident confirms</a> and <a href="/articles/should-you-stockpile-food-shortage-rumours/">how to respond to food-shortage rumours without panic buying</a>.</p><h2>How to read the briefings</h2><ul><li>Start with the release date and the period actually assessed or projected.</li><li>Check whether a count covers a whole country, selected districts, a control area or a defined population.</li><li>Keep observed results, estimates, projections and warnings distinct.</li><li>Follow the primary-source link for definitions, methodology and later updates.</li></ul><p>These pages are educational explainers, not emergency alerts or live operational dashboards. For urgent local support, use relevant official authorities and established humanitarian organizations.</p><p>See the <a href="/sources/">primary-source directory</a>, <a href="/data/">food-supply data desk</a> and <a href="/methodology/">editorial methodology</a> for wider context.</p></section></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-bottom"><span>© <span data-year>2026</span> World Food Crisis</span><span>Sources over speculation.</span></div></footer></body></html>